A||||3||pontiacs4me||one more problem, temp guage malfunction?||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||02-24-2001||03:10 AM||||Ok as you may of heard I got the 87 running and driving today with the 2.8 liter.  Ok heres the problem, when the engine is cold the temp guage reads 220, and when its warmed up it goes into the red zone.  I know for a fact its not that hot.  Ok heres what I was thinking.  the 2.8 came out of a 85 I used the 85's ecm, I wired the ecm into the rest of the stock harness, would the temp guage be getting a different kind of message from the 85 ecm?  Wouldnt the 87's be the same?  And then theres the tach issue.  I accelerate and it goes to 6000 rpm on the tach, no way maybe 4000 but not 6000. is that because the extra 2 cylinders firing is telling the tach 50% more rpm than it is actually turning?  What can I do to fix this?  I was thinking autometers.  thanks in advance for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||02-24-2001||01:42 PM||||The temperature gage doesn't come from the ECM. The ECM and gage use seperate senders. You might have reversed them, but since the wire colors are different, I doubt it. S<P>Something else is wrong.<P>On the tach as long as they are both from the correct engine (L4 or V6), they will work correctly. If the tach is from an L4, you can adjust it by finding the 25054339 chip in the tach board, cut the traces at pins 5 and 8 and solder in a 50k pot between pins 5 and 8 of the chip and adjust the tach. You need a scanner to read the ECM tach RPM or a tachometer.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||02-24-2001||05:09 PM||||Yeah! See how easy that is!?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||02-24-2001||05:51 PM||||It's intuitively obvious to the most causal observer....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>It's not the ECM making your gauge wrong.<P>You said you rewired the ECM. Maybe you swapped the wires to the two senders. Maybe not. Maybe so, but maybe not. Maybe.<P>You could have a bad connection to the sender for the gauge. A bit of corrosion will throw it off.<P>Your gauge could be bad.<P>You could be overheating and in denial. Denial is the first symptom.<P>There may be a distortion in time-space.<P>On the tach, get the right one or find someone with a solder iron and show them this thread. They'll understand.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
